    "title": "Employee Management API - Payroll Basic Module ## Learning Goal Design the Payroll Basic module of a real-world Employee Management API from features to endpoints, database ideas, validation, service flow, and diagrams. ## Simple Explanation The Payroll Basic module is one business area of the Employee Management API. A strong API design starts with user needs, then turns them into endpoints, tables, request/response DTOs, validation rules, and service logic. বাংলা সারাংশ: এই পাঠটি সহজভাবে ধারণা, ব্যবহার, ভুল এবং বাস্তব অনুশীলন বুঝতে সাহায্য করবে। ## Feature List - Generate monthly payroll - View payslip - Approve payroll batch ## API Endpoint List - `/api/payroll/runs` - `/api/payroll/payslips/{employeeId}` - `/api/payroll/runs/{id}/approve` ## Database Table Idea - `PayrollRuns`: stores payrollruns data. - `PayrollItems`: stores payrollitems data. ## Request/Response Example Request: ```json { \"name\": \"Example Name\", \"description\": \"Sample request\" } ``` Response: ```json { \"id\": 1, \"message\": \"Operation completed successfully\" } ``` ## Validation Rules - Required fields must not be empty. - Text fields should have maximum length limits. - Foreign keys must reference existing records. - Business rules should be checked in the service layer. - Unauthorized users must not access protected module operations. ## Service Flow 1. Controller receives request DTO. 2. Validator checks shape and basic rules. 3. Service checks business rules and permissions. 4. Repository reads or writes database records. 5. Service returns response DTO. 6. Controller returns the correct HTTP status code. ## Simple Explanation For this module, keep controller code small. The service should own the business decision, and the repository should own data access. This separation makes the project easier to test and maintain. ## Real-world Example In a company HR system, the Payroll Basic module supports daily HR operations while keeping employee data consistent and auditable. ## Code Example ```csharp [ApiController] [Route(\"api/payroll-basic\")] public sealed class PayrollBasicController : ControllerBase { private readonly IPayrollBasicService service; public PayrollBasicController(IPayrollBasicService service) { this.service = service; } [HttpPost] public async Task<IActionResult> Create(CreatePayrollBasicRequest request) { var result = await service.CreateAsync(request); return CreatedAtAction(nameof(Create), result); } } ``` ## Common Mistakes - Designing endpoints before understanding the business workflow. - Mixing unrelated module responsibilities in one controller. - Skipping validation because the frontend already validates. - Forgetting audit fields such as CreatedAt and UpdatedAt. ## Best Practices - Keep module boundaries clear. - Use request and response DTOs. - Add indexes for common search fields. - Log important business events. - Write tests for service rules. ## Practice Task Create one extra endpoint for the Payroll Basic module. Define the URL, HTTP method, request body, response body, validation rules, and service method name. ## Mermaid Diagram ```mermaid flowchart TD UI[Frontend or Mobile App] --> Controller[Payroll Basic Controller] Controller --> Validator[Request Validation] Validator --> Service[Payroll Basic Service] Service --> Repository[Payroll Basic Repository] Repository --> DB[(PayrollRuns, PayrollItems)] Service --> Response[Response DTO] Response --> UI ```",
